        "pt": "[NEEDS TRANSLATION]"
      },
      "duration": {
        "en": 14,
        "es": 16,
        "pt": 15
      },
      "prompt": {
        "en": "When is 'I can't' true?",
        "es": "[NEEDS TRANSLATION]",
        "pt": "[NEEDS TRANSLATION]"
      },
      "options": [
        {
          "letter": "A",
          "text": {
            "en": "Most 'I can'ts' are 'I haven'ts'—real physical limits exist, but most personal limits are beliefs mistaken for facts; distinguishing actual limits from believed limits opens possibility",
            "es": "[NEEDS TRANSLATION]",
            "pt": "[NEEDS TRANSLATION]"
          },
          "quality": "best",
          "response": {
            "en": "Yes. Some limits are real—I can't fly by flapping my arms. But most personal 'can'ts' are untested assumptions. You haven't tried everything. You haven't practiced enough. The limit might be belief, not reality.",
            "es": "[NEEDS TRANSLATION]",
            "pt": "[NEEDS TRANSLATION]"
          }
        },
        {
          "letter": "B",
          "text": {
            "en": "Some things are just not for me",
            "es": "[NEEDS TRANSLATION]",
            "pt": "[NEEDS TRANSLATION]"
          },
          "quality": "good",
          "response": {
            "en": "Fit matters—not everything is worth pursuing. But the question is: can't, or don't want to enough to do the work?...",
            "es": "[NEEDS TRANSLATION]",
            "pt": "[NEEDS TRANSLATION]"
          }
        }
      ]
    },
